/// What a project's root receives as `packageUsing:`'s argument: every
/// module the project file names, answered by the name its source declares
/// (docs/design/image-format.md, "Packaging"). A unary send of a module's
/// name answers that module's definition, found by `doesNotUnderstand:`
/// in the two arrays the factory was given. Not privileged: it holds no
/// mirror and is compiled into the program like any module, so an
/// application may keep it.
///
/// @factory makes the manifest over `moduleNames` and `moduleDefinitions`,
/// keeping both arrays as they are, neither copied nor checked; the host
/// makes one per assembled application.
/// @param moduleNames an Array of Symbols, each module's name as its source
/// declares it, in module order.
/// @param moduleDefinitions an Array of the modules' definitions, the class
/// objects (§6.4), not instances; the one at an index is the definition
/// of the name at the same index of `moduleNames`, and it is as long.
class Manifest names: moduleNames definitions: moduleDefinitions = (
  |
    /// The modules' names, the Array the factory was given: searched from
    /// the first to the last, never written.
    private names = moduleNames.
    /// The modules' definitions, the Array the factory was given: the one
    /// at an index belongs to the name at the same index, never written.
    private definitions = moduleDefinitions.
  |
) (
  /// Answers the definition of the module whose name is `message`'s
  /// selector, the first such name in module order; a message naming no
  /// module is passed to the superclass's `doesNotUnderstand:`. Only a send
  /// the manifest does not understand reaches it, so a module named like a
  /// message `Object` understands is answered by that message, never by
  /// this lookup.
  ///
  /// @param message the `Message` nothing understood; its selector is
  /// compared with each name by `=`, its arguments are ignored.
  /// @answers the module definition, a class object, when a name matches;
  /// otherwise what the superclass's `doesNotUnderstand:` answers: false
  /// for a unary selector that begins with `isKindOf`.
  /// @signals MessageNotUnderstood through the superclass's
  /// `doesNotUnderstand:`, carrying `message` and the receiver, for a
  /// selector that names no module, unless it is a unary selector that
  /// begins with `isKindOf`.
  /// @see `Object`'s `doesNotUnderstand:`.
  protected doesNotUnderstand: message = (
    |
      index ::= 1.
    |
    [index <= names size] whileTrue: [
      ((names at: index) = message selector) ifTrue: [^definitions at: index].
      index:: index + 1.
    ].
    ^super doesNotUnderstand: message.
  )
)
